    "title": "Stock Market News, March 30, 2026: U.S. Crude Pushes Above $100, Nasdaq Retreats",
    "text": "Oil prices extended gains Monday on signs that the war in Iran could last longer.Benchmark U.S. oil futures gained $3.24 a barrel Monday to settle at $102.88, their first close above $100 since 2022 when Russia invaded Ukraine and rattled energy markets.Together, those developments have driven oil prices higher. Brent crude futures for May delivery were as high as $116 Monday, reflecting tight supply, before fading back to $112.78, up slightly on the day.U.S. stocks wavered after a bruising selloff last week that put both the Dow industrials and Nasdaq composite in correction territory.Stocks gave up early gains and turned lower in afternoon trading. The Nasdaq composite finished 0.7% lower, the S&P 500 fell 0.4% and the Dow industirals rose 0.1%.Meanwhile, bond yields fell across the globe, as traders shifted their focus from the inflationary effects of the war to its impact on economic growth. The 10-year Treasury slid to 4.342%, while traders slightly pared back expectations of an interest-rate hike.Federal Reserve Chair Jerome Powell said in a speech at Harvard University that the central bank is inclined to hold rates steady and look past the energy shock from the war in Iran but cautioned that it might not be able to sit on the sidelines if rising prices shift the public’s expectations about inflation over time.",
